{
  "concept": "A Fibonacci heap is a circular doubly linked list of min-heap-ordered trees, and its abstract tree picture and its concrete four-pointer-per-node representation are the same structure viewed at two levels of abstraction.",
  "figureType": "network_diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Panel label '(a)' at left of the upper diagram",
    "Panel label '(b)' at left of the lower diagram",
    "H.min pointer label in italic serif with a downward arrow above root node 3 (one instance per panel)",
    "Root list nodes, left to right: 23, 7, 3, 17, 24 — light gray filled circles with black numerals and black outlines",
    "Panel (a) dashed horizontal line connecting the five root nodes (root list shown abstractly)",
    "Panel (a) solid tree edges: 3→18, 3→52, 3→38; 18→39; 38→41; 17→30; 24→26, 24→46; 26→35",
    "Marked nodes 18, 39, 26 drawn as solid black circles with bold white numerals in BOTH panels",
    "Unmarked internal nodes 52, 38, 41, 30, 46, 35 as light gray circles",
    "Panel (b) root-list linked structure: two concentric rounded 'racetrack' wrap-around paths enclosing the root list, one carrying rightward (next/right) arrows, one carrying leftward (prev/left) arrows, forming a circular doubly linked list",
    "Panel (b) child-list racetracks for each sibling group: {18,52,38}, {30}, {26,46}, {39}, {41}, {35} — each a circular doubly linked list, including single-node lists that loop back to themselves",
    "Panel (b) vertical double arrows between a parent and one child (parent's child pointer down, child's parent pointer up) for 3↔18, 17↔30, 24↔26, 18↔39, 38↔41, 26↔35",
    "Panel (b) diagonal upward parent pointers from 52 and 38 to 3, and from 46 to 24",
    "Legend/annotation text: 'gray = unmarked, black = marked (has lost one child since becoming a child)'",
    "Optional highlight overlay: colored halos and pointer labels 'p', 'child', 'left', 'right' when a node is selected",
    "Optional inserted node (e.g. key 21) drawn in a highlight color during the insert animation, with its two new root-list pointer arrows"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "viewMode",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Representation",
      "options": ["Abstract tree (a)", "Pointer diagram (b)", "Side by side"],
      "default": "Abstract tree (a)",
      "teaches": "The dashed root line and plain tree edges of the textbook picture are shorthand for circular doubly linked sibling lists plus parent/child pointers — same heap, two levels of detail."
    },
    {
      "id": "inspectNode",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "Every node carries exactly four pointers — p, child, left, right — and in a one-node sibling list left and right point back to the node itself, which is why splicing is always O(1)."
    },
    {
      "id": "insertAnim",
      "type": "animation",
      "label": "FIB-HEAP-INSERT(H, 21)",
      "loop": false,
      "teaches": "Insertion creates a singleton tree, rewires only four pointers to splice it into the root list, and compares one key against H.min — constant time, no consolidation."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The abstract view",
      "narration": "Five min-heap-ordered trees hang from a root list drawn as a dashed line; H.min points at the smallest root, 3. Black nodes 18, 39 and 26 are marked — each has lost one child since it became a child of its current parent.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Abstract tree (a)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Same heap, real pointers",
      "narration": "Switch to panel (b): the dashed root line becomes a circular doubly linked list, and every sibling group — even single children like 30 and 35 — becomes its own circular list with left and right arrows.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Pointer diagram (b)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Four pointers per node",
      "narration": "Click node 18, then 52, then 30. Each node shows p up to its parent, child down to just one child, and left/right around its sibling ring; a lone child's left and right point to itself.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Pointer diagram (b)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Compare the two views",
      "narration": "Side by side, the tree edge 3—52 in (a) is revealed in (b) as 52's parent pointer plus membership in the sibling ring that 3's single child pointer reaches.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Side by side" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Insert in O(1)",
      "narration": "Play FIB-HEAP-INSERT(H, 21): the new node becomes a one-node tree, four pointers splice it into the root list, and since 21 > 3 the H.min pointer does not move. No tree is ever touched.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Pointer diagram (b)" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "A Fibonacci heap is lazy because its shape lives entirely in pointers: adding or removing a tree is just relinking a circular doubly linked list, so insert costs O(1) and all the real work is deferred to extract-min.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Two stacked panels sharing the same horizontal node positions. Nodes are equal-radius circles, light gray fill with thin black stroke, black serif numerals; marked nodes 18, 39, 26 are solid black with bold white numerals. Panel (a): root row 23, 7, 3, 17, 24 joined by a single dashed horizontal line; thin solid straight edges fan down from 3 to 18/52/38, from 24 to 26/46, from 17 to 30, and single edges 18→39, 38→41, 26→35, with depth increasing downward. An italic 'H.min' label with a short down arrow sits above node 3 in both panels. Panel (b) repeats the identical layout but replaces each sibling grouping with a pair of stadium-shaped wrap-around tracks — an upper track carrying rightward arrowheads, a lower track carrying leftward arrowheads — that enclose the group and loop around its ends; single-node lists get small self-looping tracks above and below. Between generations draw pairs of nearly vertical arrows (parent's child pointer down, child's parent pointer up) and diagonal parent-only arrows from the non-first children (52, 38 to 3; 46 to 24). Use a single accent color (e.g. orange/blue) only for click highlights, pointer labels p/child/left/right, and the newly inserted node 21 during the animation; the base figure stays black, white, and gray."
}

